50413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150413 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "284d2c18-88a4-50e8-a2d2-75fa19b0e1f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150417 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d5747382-778d-5aef-bc47-1305a53de870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150418 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fed1b810-85ef-5011-b00f-2930e8117be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151914 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151914 #edb/ref 1071 "Jamie's family drove $15$ miles on $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of a gallon of gas. What is the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67b3b574-0346-5133-9f0b-303bc00ace78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151914 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas, we divide the number of miles, $15,$ by the number of gallons of gas, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{4} & =15\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 4}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $20$ miles per gallon of gas." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151915 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151915 #edb/ref 1071 "Bill measured $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ tablespoon of olive oil and found that it weighed $4$ grams. How many tablespoons is $1$ gram of olive oil?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d6f2a78-51e4-55cf-9c75-79a4624d630c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151915 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of tablespoons in $1$ gram of olive oil, we can compute the unit rate of tablespoons per gram olive oil. We do this by dividing the number of tablespoons, $\\dfrac{1}{3},$ by the number of grams, $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div 4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷4 & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of a tablespoon in $1$ gram of olive oil." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151916 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151916 #edb/ref 1071 "Martha swam $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of a mile in $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ of an hour. How many miles can Martha swim in $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f830d531-b8f0-5b62-88a5-38edf6459b55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151916 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of miles that Martha can swim in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of hours, $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{4\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martha can swim $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}$ miles in $1$ hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of an Integer and a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152104 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e3e8809-2164-543d-bb0a-c6a20cc7de6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of a Fraction and an Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152105 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b04f86ac-4028-5df6-8913-44ef091ed396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of a Fraction and a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152106 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "87385cd2-8143-54de-9995-e1cc2ecf06c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1045 "Unit Rates Associated With Ratios of Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186152988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152308 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "867309ee-c854-554c-b2ec-82c116f57f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1071 "A skydiver falls $40$ feet in $\\frac{5}{6}$ seconds. What is the unit rate of feet fallen per second?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d1cba23-ee84-559a-950a-b7d528c5c6c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152646 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of feet per second, we divide the number of feet, $40,$ by the number of seconds, $\\frac{5}{6}$, as follows: \n\n$$\n40÷\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40÷\\frac{5}{6} & =40\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\times 6}{1\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{240}{5} \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $48$ feet per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1071 "A pump moves $\\frac{9}{10}$ gallons of water in three minutes. Find the unit rate of gallons per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea2bddd6-202c-5cca-ba91-9cd2b70baf28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152647 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the unit rate of gallons per minute, we divide the number of gallons, $\\frac{9}{10},$ by the number of minutes, $3$, as follows:   \n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{10}÷3\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}÷3 & =\\frac{9}{10}÷\\frac{3}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 1}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{10\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times \\cancel{3}}{10\\times \\cancel{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate in this situation is $\\frac{3}{10}$ gallons per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1071 "Jim ran $\\frac{5}{6}$ of a mile in $\\frac{1}{5}$ of an hour. How many miles can Jim run in $1$ hour?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2761f039-d13a-562d-9bd0-6270f04a74fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152651 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of miles Jim can run in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\frac{5}{6},$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{5},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}÷\\frac{1}{5} & =\\frac{5}{6}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{6\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jim can run $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ miles in $1$ hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152976 #edb/ref 1061 16261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae0023c5-cca8-5fd0-91af-d8282fa626c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152976 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152977 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152977 #edb/ref 1061 16262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152977 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b658789-5260-5641-a7c7-e20247b9fe57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152977 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152978 #edb/ref 1061 16263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7d1d769-c6b4-5bf2-9949-638b782007aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152988 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152988 #edb/ref 1061 16684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152988 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33197a11-fb17-59b2-b2dc-f9c9927714b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152988 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153341 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100÷15 & =\\frac{100}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{100÷5}{15÷5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run $6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ meters per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153341 #edb/ref 1065 3073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153341 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9f08d42f-43a3-595e-92b3-d634f656ce2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153341 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556430 #edb/ref 1080 "$38$ pages" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186556430 #edb/ref 1083